essbook><name>OTSUKA CHEMICAL CO., LTD.</name></addressbook></applicant><applicant status="new" format="intermediate"><addressbook><name>TADA, YUJI</name></addressbook></applicant><applicant status="new" format="intermediate"><addressbook><name>YABUHARA, TADAO</name></addressbook></applicant></applicants><inventors><inventor status="new" format="epo"><addressbook><name>NAKACHO YOSHIFUMI</name><address><country>JP</country></address></addressbook></inventor><inventor status="new" format="epo"><addressbook><name>NISHIOKA YOICHI</name><address><country>JP</country></address></addressbook></inventor><inventor status="new" format="epo"><addressbook><name>TADA YUJI</name><address><country>JP</country></address></addressbook></inventor><inventor status="new" format="epo"><addressbook><name>YABUHARA TADAO</name><address><country>JP</country></address></addressbook></inventor><inventor status="new" format="intermediate"><addressbook><name>NAKACHO, YOSHIFUMI</name></addressbook></inventor><inventor status="new" format="intermediate"><addressbook><name>NISHIOKA, YOICHI</name></addressbook></inventor><inventor status="new" format="intermediate"><addressbook><name>TADA, YUJI</name></addressbook></inventor><inventor status="new" format="intermediate"><addressbook><name>YABUHARA, TADAO</name></addressbook></inventor></inventors></parties></bibliographic-data><abstract load-source="docdb" source="national office" status="new" lang="EN"><p>A flame retardant which has advantages, for example, that it is halogen-free, has a high melting point, is lowly volatile, and does not impair the properties inherent in resins. The flame retardant comprises a cross-linked phenoxyphosphazene compound which is a compound obtained by cross-linking a phosphazene compound with cross-linking groups, e.g., phenylene groups, each cross-linking group being interposed between two oxygen atoms left by phenyl group elimination, and which has no free hydroxyl group (derived from the cross-linking group) in the molecule and contains phenyl groups in an amount of 50 to 99.9 % based on the total number of all the phenyl groups in the phosphazene compound.</p></abstract><abstract load-source="docdb" source="national office" status="new" lang="FR"><p>L'invention concerne un retardeur de flamme, présentant par exemple l'avantage d'être exempt d'halogène, possédant un point de fusion élevé, faiblement volatil, et n'altérant pas les propriétés inhérentes aux résines. Ce retardeur de flamme comprend un composé de phénoxyphosphazène réticulé, obtenu par réticulation d'un composé de phosphazène avec des groupes de réticulation, par exemple des groupes phénylène, chaque groupe de réticulation étant interposé entre deux atomes d'oxygène résultant de l'élimination d'un groupe phényle, ne possédant pas de groupe hydroxyle libre (dérivé du groupe de réticulation) dans la molécule, et contenant des groupes phényle dans une quantité de 50 % à 99 % sur la base du nombre total de groupes phényle du composé de phosphazène.</p></abstract><copyright>User acknowledges that the Information Retrieval Facility (IRF) and its third party providers retain all right, title and interest in and to this xml under applicable copyright laws. User acquires no ownership rights to this xml including but not limited to its format. User hereby accepts the terms and conditions of the Licence Agreement set forth at http://www.ir-facility.org/legal/marec/data_licence</copyright></patent-document>